  "sub_agent_results": {
   "materials": {
    "analysis": "A Brazilian government bond may provide a modest hedge against inflation, but I would not recommend it as a primary commodity hedge. While it is a domestic security, it is a debt instrument and does not directly provide exposure to inflation. However, it has a relatively high correlation with Brazilian inflation, which can serve as an inflation hedge. Nevertheless, its exposure to currency fluctuations (Brazilian real) and credit risk means it is not a direct hedge against inflation. A more direct and effective inflation hedge in this context would be Gold or Silver. Considering the inflation risks, I recommend a modest allocation to these precious metals. Inflation correlation rationale: both Gold and Silver exhibit a high positive correlation with inflation due to their historically stable purchasing power and ability to maintain value during periods of high inflation.",
    "constraint_flags": [],
    "recommendation": "hold",
    "confidence": "high",
    "proposed_allocation": [
     0.1,
     0.05
    ],
    "out_of_scope": false
   },
   "bonds": {
    "analysis": "Brazilian government bonds have a high credit risk due to Brazil's history ofDefaults on bonds. As such, they are unsuitable for inclusion in our investment portfolio, which requires investment-grade securities (min_credit_rating: BBB+). A laddered maturity structure must be employed to manage the portfolio duration, ensuring that no more than 30% of bonds mature in any single year. Given the portfolio's focus on stability and predictable cash flows, government bonds from countries with improved credit profiles would be a better fit. Our proposed allocation should instead focus on investment-grade sovereign bonds with a laddered maturity structure, such as US or German bonds.",
